A component with state of its own gets a cell per copy under a loop over a
const, as it does under a loop over state.

`counter` declares `hits`, and a list of three labels renders three of them.
Every target but one keeps an instance's state itself -- a record on fyne and
gtk4, a factory closure on html, `remember` on android -- and builds the
counter at run time, so each row is an instance with its own `hits`.
bubbletea is the exception: an Elm model keeps every cell in itself, and a
component built at run time there is a render function with nowhere to put
one. So it splices the counter into the loop and gives `hits` a cell per copy,
a map keyed by the row's index, which the Enter a focused row receives reads
and writes through.

Before, the loop's iterable being a const decided it: the body was spliced once
and `hits` hoisted once, and every row counted the same clicks.

The click writes `hits` twice, which on bubbletea is two reads of the copy's
cell into a temporary and two stores back, spliced into one Go block: the two
temporaries were one name, declared twice.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `\thits__inst\d+ +int\b` -- the rows' counts are a cell per copy, not one int every row writes
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `m\.__n\d+ = newCounterInstance` -- each row is an instance kept by the slot, not one Model field every row overwrites
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `m\.__n\d+ = newCounterInstance` -- each row is an instance kept by the slot, not one Model field every row overwrites

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

const labels list[string] = ["a", "b", "c"]

component counter(label = "") node {
    var hits = 0
    button(text="{label} {hits}", @click {
        hits = hits + 1
        hits = hits % 10
    })
}

window {
    vbox {
        for var l = labels {
            counter(label=l)
        }
    }
}
